WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.853 --> 00:00:02.702
(screams)

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:03.670
- Kevin!

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:06.100
- [Narrator] Home Alone
redefined Christmas movies,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.490
but for the people who made this mega hit,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.600
it was no holiday.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.156
- He didn't wanna hear my ideas,
he didn't wanna talk to me.

00:00:12.156 --> 00:00:13.050
(gun fires)

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.657
- [Narrator] This is the
surprisingly painful story

00:00:15.657 --> 00:00:19.153
of one of movie history's
greatest Christmas miracles.

00:00:19.153 --> 00:00:20.090
(shovel thuds)
(hollers)

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.350
- Bob Daly said, all right, well,

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.630
if you can do this movie
for 10, we'll make it.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.630
- [Narrator] 10 million
might sound like a lot,

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.737
but compared to most movies
that came out in the late 80s,

00:00:29.737 --> 00:00:31.930
$10 million was nothing.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.990
So you might be wondering
how they pulled off

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.520
all that stunt work, star power.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.700
- Polka king of the Midwest.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.232
- [Narrator] And pyrotechnics.

00:00:39.232 --> 00:00:40.065
(screams)

00:00:40.065 --> 00:00:41.970
- [Narrator] For only $10 million.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.133
Well, they didn't.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.240
New Trier wasn't just any old high school.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.140
That's right, the ghost
of John Hughes' films past

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.730
inhabited the halls,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.380
being a location for
Ferris Bueller's Day Off

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.110
and Uncle Buck.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.220
- We walked into this
gym and we were like,

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.980
it's got a grid in the
ceiling, and it's big enough,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.869
and we could build a house
in here, I would think.

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:10.130
(hammer pounding)

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.740
And the next thing we knew,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.380
we built all the sets in this school.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.870
It was a beautifully built,

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.120
structurally built, two floors,
so you could walk up steps.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.890
Actually, the front door we shot here.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.320
- We're the wet bandits.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.330
- You know, the trueism is
any time you build a set

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.880
with water in it, it's gonna leak.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.790
- I mean, it is what it is, right?

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.671
We have to have a set, we
have to have it flooded,

00:01:30.671 --> 00:01:32.350
where are we gonna do it?

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.670
- [Narrator] The answer
was down this hall,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.203
past the canteen, through the
auditorium, and in this door.

00:01:40.686 --> 00:01:43.150
- The pool, the pool,
it has water in it now.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.637
And it's like, well it
may as well drip into

00:01:45.637 --> 00:01:47.750
an empty pool, right?

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.552
(dramatic music)

00:01:50.552 --> 00:01:52.448
So we built the set
down at the shallow end.

00:01:52.448 --> 00:01:54.433
Yes, that is correct.

00:01:55.269 --> 00:01:56.220
(dramatic music)

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.190
- It was such a fun project and
there was so much enthusiasm

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.533
for it that even though
the budget was climbing.

00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:04.224
- 11, 92, 12.

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:05.970
- 13 million to 14 million.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.020
- John believed that Warner
Brothers would be comfortable

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.820
with whatever budget that it ended up at.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:12.840
- [Narrator] An eerie feeling

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.900
began haunting the vacant school.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.760
- John finally came and
said, they're having issues

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.800
with the budget.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.240
- Unless we could deliver

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.570
a 13 and a half million
dollar budget the next day

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.530
they were gonna pull the plug.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.980
- This is Warner Brothers,
aren't we close enough,

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.440
I mean, it's not like we said it was 25.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.540
I hang the phone and I had
seen the production manager

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.804
from Warner Brothers going to each office

00:02:35.804 --> 00:02:38.807
telling everybody the project is dead.

00:02:38.807 --> 00:02:43.807
Close up, pack up, leave, go home.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:49.120
- [Narrator] And with
that, Home Alone was dead.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.480
- We were walking back from the commissary

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.870
and we ask him, how's
it going on Home Alone

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.300
'cause we heard about it

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:01.030
and John said well, I'm actually
fighting with the studios

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:02.383
and Warner Brothers.

00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:05.817
- [Narrator] And thus began a
legally questionable exchange.

00:03:05.817 --> 00:03:06.650
- Who is it?

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:09.520
- [Narrator] Between John
Hughes and 20th Century Fox.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.300
- Legally another studio
isn't supposed to see

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.982
a piece of material until
it's legally in turn around

00:03:16.982 --> 00:03:19.460
and that didn't exactly happen.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.770
- Leave it on the doorstep
and get the hell out of here.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.490
- Basically a screen
play was left somewhere

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:26.300
so that somebody could pick it up.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.040
It was clandestinely delivered.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.750
Click, pick up the phone, dial Fox

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:34.230
and say we got the call and they said

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.763
you're now a Fox picture.

00:03:35.763 --> 00:03:37.440
(dramatic music)

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.010
- [Narrator] A valid question was.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.620
- How much snow, did we have enough money

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.750
in the budget for snow.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:44.840
- But the heavens provided
a Christmas miracle.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:48.350
- I remember the day kind of
started to rain a little bit

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:50.596
and then it just dumped.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:52.311
(dramatic music)

00:03:52.311 --> 00:03:53.210
- And our beepers started going off saying

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.620
we're moving to the McCallister house

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.950
first thing in the
morning, make it happen.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.300
- [Narrator] On just their
second day of shooting

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.510
they nailed one of the most
important shots in the movie.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.262
- The money shot, that's what
it was it was the money shot.

00:04:07.262 --> 00:04:10.020
- The snow was fantastic, it was more snow

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.820
than we really needed but we
weren't gonna mess with it.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.190
- [Narrator] But their
little snowy miracle

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:15.910
came with a side of trimmings.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.890
- We used potato flakes
because we needed snow blowing,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:23.220
within a day or two when
the snow started to melt,

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.510
the potato flakes started to degrade

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.000
and it became like this
horrible funky vegetable smell.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.443
- [Narrator] So two days
into shooting it dumped

00:04:32.443 --> 00:04:34.870
and things were turning rotten.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.040
To star alongside Pesci they cast Daniel.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.350
- Daniel.
- Daniel.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:40.803
- Daniel.
- Daniel.

00:04:40.803 --> 00:04:41.729
- Dan Roebuck.

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:42.840
- [Narrator] Roebuck.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.010
- And then we did a screen
test with that actor

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.170
and Joe Pesci.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.250
- I don't think Joe felt
like they had chemistry.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.972
- They just felt a little flat.

00:04:50.972 --> 00:04:54.170
- And after three days,
Chris came into our office

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.880
and said guys we have
to replace Dan Roebuck,

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.960
it's not working.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.925
- I really need to try to get
Dan Stern into this movie.

00:05:01.925 --> 00:05:04.370
- That's a good idea.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.420
- Then when we did the
camera test with Joe Pesci

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.266
and Dan Stern, it was magical.

00:05:09.266 --> 00:05:11.500
- Does that bother you when I do that?

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.333
- Nope.

00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:13.758
- You know you could get locked up for--

00:05:13.758 --> 00:05:17.450
- The hardest thing for
Pesci was not swearing.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:18.740
- Don't (beep) me out.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:19.573
How the (beep).

00:05:19.573 --> 00:05:20.573
Give me a (beep) break.

00:05:20.573 --> 00:05:21.780
(beep) give me the drive through.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.199
- Joe said an interesting thing to me,

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:25.032
he said every time I get a script,

00:05:25.032 --> 00:05:27.468
it's not a Scorsese script,
the only way I can read it

00:05:27.468 --> 00:05:30.683
is if I add (beep) every
three or four words.

00:05:32.068 --> 00:05:32.901
- [Narrator] Although it's
a wholesome family film

00:05:32.901 --> 00:05:34.993
one might excuse the odd swear word.

00:05:34.993 --> 00:05:38.250
(hollers)
- What?

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.867
- And he said I wanna make
a language like a (mumbles).

00:05:42.213 --> 00:05:44.346
- What?
(mumbles)

00:05:44.346 --> 00:05:46.270
- What, what?
- What happened!

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.602
- Like a cartoon rough guy.

00:05:49.602 --> 00:05:51.940
- [Narrator] Everybody
looked forward to the day

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.715
when John Candy would be on set.

00:05:53.715 --> 00:05:56.320
♪ Holka polka polka ♪

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.710
- I don't think John Candy knows

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:58.920
it was gonna be 23 hours.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.550
- And here was a point
where Candy looked at John

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:05.440
and it was like, you son of a (beep).

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.110
- Maybe we shouldn't talk about it.

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:08.720
- [Narrator] For a 23 hour day,

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.320
an actor as big as John Candy
must have made a fortune

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.760
in overtime or something.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.890
- John Candy did that as a favor for us.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.380
- Sure, you know, it's Christmas time.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.750
- There wasn't some big
salary, it was scale.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.370
- [Narrator] Yep, that's right,
Dan Charles, the pizza boy

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.930
made more than John Candy on Home Alone.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.190
- Cheapskate.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:26.510
- When I did the first stair fall,

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.350
no one told me how to take the fall,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.656
I just launched myself as
high and as far as I could.

00:06:31.656 --> 00:06:32.710
(hollers)

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:33.820
- [Narrator] The falls in Home Alone

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.859
had a huge impact on the stunt industry.

00:06:36.859 --> 00:06:38.143
(hollers)

00:06:38.143 --> 00:06:41.550
- Now when somebody does a fall
where they get a lot of air

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.330
and they fall on their back,
they call it the Home Alone.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.350
(hollers)

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:49.250
- [Narrator] But there was
one other iconic moment

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.960
in the film that wasn't
exactly performed as written.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.127
(hollers)

00:06:57.647 --> 00:06:58.770
- John scripted that moment.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:02.230
He was supposed to do this
and then pause and scream

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.252
but he did this and then.

00:07:05.252 --> 00:07:07.770
He kept his hands there just

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.310
because it was such a
little kid thing to do.

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:13.570
But as a result he
created this iconic moment

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.383
by putting his hands there
and keeping them there.

